"""Chapter 4, second half: fertiliser, NDVI, plant health, assistant and layout, deployment."""

BLOCKS = [
    ("h2", "4.6 Fertiliser planning engine"),
    ("p", "The fertiliser engine turns the farmer's own soil-test numbers into amounts of common fertilisers and dates for applying them. It needs no sensor and no laboratory link. The farmer types the available nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium from a soil report or a Soil Health Card in kilograms per hectare, and may leave any of them blank. {fig:nutflow} shows the steps."),
    ("fig", "nutflow", "figures/diagrams/nutrient_flow.png", "Steps of the fertiliser plan, from soil-test values to fertiliser amounts and dates.", 6.3),
    ("p", "Each value is rated against the Soil Health Card limits [@shc], given in {tab:limits}. A blank value is rated unknown. The crop's general dose of N, P₂O₅ and K₂O in kg/ha is then raised by a quarter when the rating is low, cut by a quarter when it is high, and left unchanged when it is medium or unknown, which is the usual ICAR soil-test adjustment. With G the general dose of nutrient n, A the factor 1.25, 1.00 or 0.75 from {tab:limits}, and D the adjusted dose:"),
    ("tab", "limits", "Soil Health Card limits used for ratings (kg/ha)",
     ["Nutrient", "Low if below", "High if above", "Dose factor (low / medium / high)"],
     [["Available nitrogen (N)", "280", "560", "1.25 / 1.00 / 0.75"],
      ["Olsen phosphorus (P)", "10", "25", "1.25 / 1.00 / 0.75"],
      ["Ammonium-acetate potassium (K)", "110", "280", "1.25 / 1.00 / 0.75"]],
     [3200, 1800, 1800, 3367], ["left", "center", "center", "center"]),
    ("eq", "dose", r"D_n=\text{round}\left(G_n\cdot A_n\right)"),
    ("p", "The nutrients are then converted to products. Di-ammonium phosphate (DAP, 18-46-0) supplies the phosphorus and part of the nitrogen, urea (46% N) supplies the rest of the nitrogen, and muriate of potash (MOP, 60% K₂O) supplies potassium. Multiplying by the field area gives the quantity to buy:"),
    ("eq", "products", r"\cases{\text{DAP}=\frac{D_P}{0.46} @@ \text{Urea}=\frac{\max\left(0,\;D_N-0.18\cdot\text{DAP}\right)}{0.46} @@ \text{MOP}=\frac{D_K}{0.60}}".replace(r"\;", "")),
    ("p", "Nitrogen is split over the season. Legumes fix their own nitrogen and receive all of it at sowing. Crops with seasons longer than 200 days, such as sugarcane and turmeric, get four equal parts at planting and at 15%, 30% and 50% of the season. Other crops get half at sowing and a quarter each at 30% and 55% of the season. The engine prints the dates from the planting date. The general doses are indicative national figures, and the six crops whose doses were matched to a published guide (sorghum, pearl millet, sesame, castor, onion and coriander) are flagged as checked; the others are flagged as typical values. The farmer is told that a Soil Health Card dose or local extension advice should override these numbers."),
    ("h2", "4.7 Satellite crop-health module"),
    ("p", "The crop-health module reports how green each part of the field looks from space. It rests on NDVI, which compares the near-infrared and red bands [@huang]. For Sentinel-2 the near-infrared band is B08 and the red band is B04:"),
    ("eq", "ndvi", r"\text{NDVI}=\frac{B_{08}-B_{04}}{B_{08}+B_{04}}"),
    ("p", "{fig:ndviflow} shows the steps. The boundary is converted to a closed GeoJSON polygon, and fields under 1,000 m² (a few 10 m pixels) are refused. The server then searches the STAC catalogue for Sentinel-2 Level-2A scenes over the field in the last 150 days with scene-wide cloud cover under 60%, keeps at most one scene per day, and requests statistics for each scene from the data service: the mean, minimum, maximum and standard deviation of NDVI inside the polygon, and a histogram. A second request returns the scene classification layer for the same polygon [@pc]."),
    ("fig", "ndviflow", "figures/diagrams/ndvi_flow.png", "Satellite crop-health pipeline from the field boundary to the farmer's summary.", 6.4),
    ("p", "A scene is usable only if fewer than 20% of the field's pixels fall in the classes saturated, cloud shadow, cloud (medium or high probability), thin cirrus or snow, and at least four pixels are valid. The check looks at the field itself, not the whole tile, so a scene with clouds elsewhere is still used. From the histogram the module computes the share of the field that is weak (NDVI below 0.3), moderate (0.3 to 0.6) or strong (0.6 and above), interpolating linearly inside a histogram bin. The mean is mapped to a plain label from very little green cover (below 0.2) to very dense vigorous cover (0.8 and above)."),
    ("p", "The summary compares the latest usable scene with an earlier usable one at least five days older and calls the trend improving, steady or declining when the mean moves by 0.05 or more. A fall of 0.10 or more produces a note to walk the field and look for water stress, pests or disease, and to expect such a drop after harvest. The module also notes when more than 20% of the field is weak, and when the spread across the field exceeds 0.15, which often means uneven water, soil or sowing. Every summary ends with the reminder that NDVI depends on crop and growth stage and does not diagnose a cause. The browser draws the colour map from the same service's tile endpoint, and the latest reading is saved on the field so that it appears on the field card and in the report. Results are cached for six hours per boundary and scenes are processed by eight parallel workers."),
    ("h2", "4.8 Plant-health module"),
    ("p", "The plant-health module identifies a likely leaf condition from a photo. It is a cascade: a local classifier answers first, because it is free and fast, and a vision language model gives a second opinion when the classifier is unsure. {fig:diseaseflow} summarises the flow."),
    ("fig", "diseaseflow", "figures/diagrams/disease_flow.png", "Cascade from the farmer's photo to a classification, a second opinion and advice.", 6.4),
    ("h3", "4.8.1 Data and labels"),
    ("p", "The classifier is trained on two public collections that were merged under a single naming scheme, Crop|Condition. PlantDoc contributes field-style web photographs and its own train and test splits [@plantdoc; @plantdoc_kaggle]. PlantVillage contributes laboratory photographs, and only its colour images are used [@pv_kaggle]. To keep laboratory images from drowning the field images, at most 100 PlantVillage images per class go into training. A label table maps folder names of both datasets to one spelling, so that, for example, Apple scab and Tomato bacterial spot are one class each, whichever collection a photo came from. The merged set has 38 classes across crops including apple, bell pepper, cherry, corn, grape, peach, potato, soybean, squash, strawberry and tomato. {tab:data} gives the split sizes."),
    ("tab", "data", "Data used to train and evaluate the plant model",
     ["Split", "Source", "Images", "Use"],
     [["Train", "PlantDoc train (85%) and PlantVillage (at most 100 per class)", "6,070", "Fit the network"],
      ["Validation", "15% of PlantDoc train, near-duplicates of training images removed", "394", "Choose the epoch, fit the temperature and thresholds"],
      ["Test", "PlantDoc test split", "252", "Report accuracy (12 images are near-duplicates of training images)"]],
     [1300, 4600, 1100, 3167], ["left", "left", "center", "left"]),
    ("p", "PlantDoc contains near-identical web images, so a validation or test photo can have an almost identical twin in training. The notebook computes a perceptual difference hash for every image and treats two images as near-duplicates when their 64-bit hashes differ in five bits or fewer. Near-duplicates are removed from the validation set, so the calibration is honest, and the test score is reported twice, on all 252 images and on the 240 that have no twin in training."),
    ("h3", "4.8.2 Network and training"),
    ("p", "The network is ConvNeXt-Tiny with ImageNet weights, and its final linear layer is replaced by one with 38 outputs [@convnext]. Images keep their aspect ratio and are resized and centre-cropped to 288 by 288 pixels, and the validation, test and serving steps use exactly the same preprocessing. Training augments each image with a random resized crop covering 35% to 100% of the area, a horizontal flip, a rotation up to 25 degrees and colour jitter. The loss is cross-entropy with label smoothing [@muller] and class weights that offset unequal class sizes. With C classes, N training images, n_{c} images in class c, smoothing ε = 0.1 and the true class y, the weights and the smoothed targets are"),
    ("eq", "cw", r"\omega_c=\frac{N}{C\cdot n_c}"),
    ("eq", "smooth", r"q_c=\left(1-\varepsilon\right)\delta_{c,y}+\frac{\varepsilon}{C},\qquad".replace(r",\qquad", "")),
    ("eq", "loss", r"L=-\omega_y\sum_{c}{q_c\ln p_c}"),
    ("p", "The optimiser is AdamW [@adamw] with a learning rate of 10⁻⁴ for the pretrained backbone and 10⁻³ for the new head, weight decay 0.05, and a cosine annealing schedule over 25 epochs. Batches hold 32 images and training uses half-precision arithmetic on a Colab T4 GPU. The checkpoint with the best accuracy on the field-photo validation set is kept. {tab:train} collects the settings."),
    ("tab", "train", "Training settings of the plant model (version 3)",
     ["Setting", "Value"],
     [["Backbone", "ConvNeXt-Tiny, ImageNet weights, about 28 million parameters"],
      ["Input size", "288 by 288 pixels, aspect ratio kept"],
      ["Classes", "38 (Crop|Condition)"],
      ["Epochs, batch size", "25, 32"],
      ["Optimiser", "AdamW, lr 1e-4 (backbone), 1e-3 (head), weight decay 0.05"],
      ["Schedule", "Cosine annealing over 25 epochs"],
      ["Loss", "Cross-entropy, label smoothing 0.1, class weights"],
      ["Augmentation", "Random resized crop (0.35 to 1.0), flip, rotation 25 degrees, colour jitter"],
      ["Test-time", "Average of the logits for the image and its mirror image"],
      ["Seed", "42"]],
     [2600, 7567]),
    ("h3", "4.8.3 Calibration and the decision to answer"),
    ("p", "Raw network scores are not probabilities. The notebook divides the logits z by a temperature T, with T found by a grid search from 0.5 to 5.0 in steps of 0.01 that minimises the cross-entropy on the validation set. The logits used at test time are the mean of the logits for the photo and its mirror image [@minderer; @gawlikowski]."),
    ("eq", "flip", r"z=\frac{1}{2}\left(f(x)+f\left(x_{\text{flip}}\right)\right)"),
    ("eq", "softT", r"p_i=\frac{\exp\left(z_i/T\right)}{\sum_{j}{\exp\left(z_j/T\right)}}"),
    ("p", "Calibration quality is measured by the expected calibration error. Predictions are placed into B equal-width bins of confidence, and the gap between the accuracy and the mean confidence of each bin is weighted by the bin's size n_{b}, with n the total number of predictions:"),
    ("eq", "ece", r"\text{ECE}=\sum_{b=1}^{B}{\frac{n_b}{n}\left|\text{acc}\left(b\right)-\text{conf}\left(b\right)\right|}"),
    ("p", "The model answers only when its top probability reaches a threshold τ. The notebook picks τ as the smallest confidence at which the accepted validation predictions reach 90% accuracy, with at least 20 predictions accepted, and it fits one threshold for the case where the farmer picks the crop and one for the case where the crop is unknown. When the crop is named, the output is restricted to that crop's classes before the softmax, which removes impossible answers. The server never trusts a threshold lower than 0.85, because on field photos a threshold of 0.74 gave about 80% accuracy and 0.85 about 85%. Two quantities describe the trade-off for the set A of accepted photos:"),
    ("eq", "cov", r"\cases{\text{Coverage}=\frac{\left|A\right|}{n} @@ \text{Accuracy}_{A}=\frac{\text{number correct in } A}{\left|A\right|}}"),
    ("h3", "4.8.4 Second opinion and advice"),
    ("p", "If the top probability is below the threshold, or the chosen crop has only a healthy class in the model, the server sends the photo to a vision language model on Groq. The photo is shrunk to at most 768 pixels and sent as a JPEG, because the free tier allows only about 8,000 tokens a minute, and the reply is limited to 800 tokens. The prompt demands one JSON object with the crop, whether the plant looks healthy, one sentence on visible symptoms, up to three possible causes ordered by likelihood with a type (disease, pest, nutrient, abiotic or unknown) and a reason, and up to three follow-up questions the farmer can answer. The prompt also tells the model to be honest when the photo is unclear and not to invent symptoms. When the free-tier limit is hit, the application says so and asks the farmer to wait about 30 seconds. If neither engine can answer, it reports that instead of guessing."),
    ("p", "A separate request asks the assistant for advice under four headings: what it is, cure and treatment, prevention, and when to see an expert, in under 220 words. The prompt forbids dosage rates and requires a recommendation to follow the product label and local extension advice, and the farmer's answers to the follow-up questions are passed in to narrow the cause. Every photo result carries the warning that it is a likely classification aid, not a confirmed diagnosis, and the results are saved in the field's history."),
    ("h2", "4.9 Assistant, field plan, reports and accessibility"),
    ("p", "The assistant sends the question and the saved field context to a Groq language model with a low temperature (0.2) and a system prompt that restricts it to the supplied context, forbids invented measurements, diagnoses, pesticide rates and yield guarantees, asks for one short follow-up when information is missing, and asks for the sections Advice, Why, What to observe and Safety. When the farmer has chosen Hindi, Marathi, Bengali or Tamil, the prompt adds an instruction to answer in that language in simple words, keeping product and chemical names in English letters too. The model name is read from the environment, so it can be changed when a free tier changes."),
    ("p", "After choosing a crop, the farmer can ask for a layout drawn on the saved boundary. The browser works in a local frame in metres with the long side of the field as the row direction, insets a 3 m headland, divides wide fields into 36 m blocks separated by 2 m working paths, and draws rows at the crop's typical spacing. For a sprinkler layout it places lines 12 m apart with sprinklers at about 12 m along each line, and for drip it places emitters 0.4 m apart along the drip lines. It reports row length, plant count and pipe length. The layout is pure geometry: it knows nothing about slope, soil or what already stands on the field, and the source code states that limit."),
    ("p", "The Reports page assembles the field profile, the boundary map, the crops chosen, the irrigation dates and the fertiliser plan into one page that the farmer prints or saves as PDF through the browser. The irrigation page also offers Google Calendar links and an `.ics` file."),
    ("p", "The menus, page titles and main buttons are available in English, Hindi, Marathi, Bengali and Tamil. The wording has not been reviewed by native speakers, a limit recorded in the project documentation. Area can be shown in hectares or acres and temperature in °C or °F, and the choice is kept on the device. Results appear one card at a time with swipe and arrow navigation. The layout adapts from a 320 px phone to a large desktop with a slide-in menu on narrow screens, finger-sized controls and no sideways scrolling."),
    ("h2", "4.10 Deployment"),
    ("p", "Dhartisense runs as one process, so any host that runs a container or a Python process can serve it. A bundle builder, `deploy/build_bundle.py`, assembles a clean folder with the server, the browser app, the engines, the two data files, a Dockerfile and a Render blueprint. It never copies keys, raw datasets, training code or the virtual environment, and it leaves out the plant model unless asked. The container image is based on Python 3.12, runs as a non-root user and reads the port from the environment. {fig:deploy} shows the path from the repository to a host."),
    ("fig", "deploy", "figures/diagrams/deployment.png", "Deployment path: repository, clean bundle, container host and managed services.", 6.2),
    ("p", "For a pilot the free Render plan needs no code change, but it has 512 MB of memory, sleeps after 15 minutes without visits and keeps no disk, which is acceptable because all data lives in Firebase. Google Cloud Run suits a custom domain and removes the sleep delay. Because PyTorch needs about 1 GB, the local plant model does not fit the free Render plan, and the default bundle uses the vision model alone for photos. The configuration is held in environment variables, and secrets such as the Firebase service-account key and the Groq key are never committed."),
]
